## Overview

Financial fraud detection is challenging because fraudulent transactions are relatively rare, fraud patterns change over time, and excessive false alarms can inconvenience legitimate customers.

This project proposes a hybrid fraud detection system that combines supervised machine learning with statistical anomaly detection, transaction-velocity analysis, and configurable rules. The system will estimate transaction risk, provide explanations, generate alerts, and recommend an appropriate action.

The platform is intended for academic research and demonstration. It does not connect to a real banking or payment network.

## Objectives

- Detect potentially fraudulent financial transactions.
- Develop a hybrid risk assessment pipeline.
- Identify unusual transaction amounts and high-frequency activity.
- Allow configurable rules and alerts.
- Provide explainable fraud predictions.
- Minimize the expected financial cost of fraud and false positives.
- Monitor changes in transaction behaviour and model performance.
- Evaluate the system using time-based validation and multiple datasets where feasible.

